Yes. Looking across the problems you repeatedly come back to, I think there **is a credible business hiding in your history**.

Not a guaranteed “make millions” idea—nobody can honestly promise that—but there is one where I think you have unusually good founder-market fit.

# The idea: an AI Engineering Memory / Operating Layer

Think:

> **A system that learns how a software team actually works, then gives AI coding agents the institutional knowledge needed to work like a member of that team.**

I would *not* build another coding agent.

Codex, Claude Code, Copilot, Cursor, etc. already write code extremely well. AI development-tool adoption is already enormous: JetBrains reported that 90% of developers regularly used at least one AI tool for development work by January 2026. ([The JetBrains Blog][1])

Instead, build the **memory and governance layer around those agents**.

And you've already independently bumped into this problem several times.

---

## Why I arrived here from your history

A pattern appears repeatedly in things you've wanted to build.

You've talked about:

* capturing PR reviewer feedback and remembering that *Joe prefers X whereas another reviewer accepts Y*
* turning that feedback into reusable coding knowledge
* having AI report what it changed and why
* automatically understanding Jira tickets
* associating GitHub PRs with Jira work
* tracking what you're working on
* reconstructing work from browser activity
* maintaining a central AI-assisted work dashboard
* having different machines/nodes exchange information
* keeping data private/local where possible
* building lightweight tools rather than enormous enterprise systems

Those aren't really separate ideas.

They're manifestations of the same missing layer:

**software teams have huge amounts of undocumented operational knowledge that AI agents don't possess.**

For example:

> "We don't use this interface here because this particular implementation needs X."

> "Rebecca normally expects this information in the Jira ticket."

> "Joe commented on three PRs that repositories using this package should implement it this way."

> "When touching the basket search, remember that POS intentionally behaves differently from the frontend."

> "This weird code exists because of SS-6160."

> "Don't 'clean up' this filter. It deliberately preserves legacy CP behaviour."

You encounter exactly this sort of knowledge constantly.

Codex doesn't inherently know it.

Claude doesn't know it.

A new developer certainly doesn't know it.

And conventional documentation tends not to capture it.

---

# The product

Give it a working name like **Lore**.

The basic proposition:

> **Lore learns your engineering organisation while you work and supplies the right organisational context to humans and AI agents at the moment they need it.**

It connects to:

```text
GitHub / GitLab
      │
Jira ─┼──────► Lore
      │          │
Slack │          ├── Engineering knowledge graph
      │          ├── Decisions
Docs ─┘          ├── Reviewer preferences
                 ├── Architectural conventions
                 ├── Historical reasoning
                 └── Project quirks
                           │
                 ┌─────────┼─────────┐
                 ▼         ▼         ▼
              Codex    Claude Code   Cursor
```

But the interesting part isn't RAG/search.

It's **learning from what happens after the AI acts**.

---

# Example from your actual workflow

Imagine Codex produces:

```php
class ProductRepository
{
    ...
}
```

Joe reviews the PR:

> Can we implement ProductRepositoryInterface here rather than depending directly on the concrete repository?

Lore observes that.

It extracts something like:

```yaml
rule:
  concept: repository-interface
  scope:
    repository: soho/ecom
  evidence:
    - pull_request: 1832
      reviewer: joe
  confidence: 0.64

guidance:
  "Repository dependencies should normally use
   their interface rather than concrete implementation."
```

Two months later another PR gets created.

Before Codex starts, Lore injects:

```text
Relevant engineering knowledge:

Repository dependencies in this project normally use
their corresponding interface.

Evidence:
PR #1832
PR #1941
PR #2017

Confidence: High
```

Now something much more interesting happens.

Joe approves it.

That is **additional evidence that the learned rule was correct**.

Lore gets smarter from the development lifecycle.

---

# And it learns *why* ugly code exists

This could be even more valuable.

Consider the kind of basket-search issue you've previously worked through.

An AI sees:

```php
if ($this->isOmsBasket()) {
    // skip active filter
}
```

and thinks:

> That's inconsistent. I'll simplify it.

Lore knows:

```text
DO NOT REMOVE

Reason:
Admin CP/POS basket searches historically allow
region-enabled + priced products even where the normal
frontend Active filter would exclude them.

Introduced during Elasticsearch migration.

Related:
SS-xxxx
PR #xxxx
```

That is extraordinarily useful with AI coding agents.

As AI writes more code, **understanding why existing code looks wrong becomes increasingly important**.

---

# Why I prefer this over simply building an AI code reviewer

That market is already becoming crowded.

[CodeRabbit](https://www.coderabbit.ai/?utm_source=chatgpt.com), for example, has just raised $143 million at a **$1.5 billion valuation**, reports more than 17,000 customers and more than two million code reviews per week. ([Reuters][2])

[GitHub](https://github.com/?utm_source=chatgpt.com) says Copilot code review has already performed more than **60 million reviews** and accounts for more than one in five code reviews on GitHub. ([The GitHub Blog][3])

So I wouldn't fight them.

I'd make them **better**.

Your positioning becomes:

> **We don't write your code. We teach your AI how your company writes code.**

That distinction matters.

---

# The moat is the team's history

This is where I think there's potentially a substantial company rather than another disposable AI wrapper.

A generic AI application is relatively easy to reproduce.

Your customer's accumulated engineering intelligence isn't.

After a company has used Lore for two years it could contain:

```text
31,482 PR decisions
8,291 reviewer corrections
1,943 architectural decisions
712 project-specific conventions
286 historical compatibility constraints
141 recurring production problems
93 undocumented integration quirks
```

Now replacing Lore means throwing away **two years of learned organisational behaviour**.

That's a real switching cost.

A recent analysis of the AI application market makes essentially this broader point: with applications becoming cheap to build, defensibility increasingly comes from proprietary data, distribution, and deep workflow integration rather than the AI model itself. ([TechRadar][4])

Your product naturally develops all three.

---

# There's another important feature: "Why?"

You should be able to select code and ask:

> **Why is this implemented this way?**

Lore searches:

Git history → PR → Jira → reviewer discussion → related PRs → architectural decisions.

Then responds:

> This was deliberately implemented this way in PR #829.
>
> The normal implementation caused inactive products to disappear from CP basket search following the Elasticsearch migration.
>
> Rebecca confirmed CP should preserve the old SQL behaviour in SS-xxxx.
>
> Changing this would potentially reintroduce that regression.

That could save engineering organisations an enormous amount of archaeology.

And you've personally spent significant amounts of time doing exactly that archaeology.

---

# Then add reviewer intelligence

This is something I particularly like from your earlier idea.

Not merely:

```text
Company coding standards
```

but:

```text
Organisation
   ↓
Team
   ↓
Repository
   ↓
Subsystem
   ↓
Reviewer
```

For example:

```text
Global:
Prefer dependency injection.

Soho repository:
Use service interfaces.

Checkout:
Avoid changing transaction state directly.

Joe:
Frequently requests interfaces around repositories.

Rebecca:
Requires integration behaviour documented in Jira.
```

The AI can distinguish **hard architectural rules from reviewer tendencies**.

That is much more sophisticated than a giant `AGENTS.md`.

---

# It also solves the stale-rules problem

You don't want Lore blindly accumulating instructions forever.

Each piece of knowledge should have:

```text
confidence
evidence count
last observed
scope
contradictions
superseded_by
source
```

Suppose ten PRs followed:

```text
Always use ProductInterface
```

Then architecture changes.

Three recent PRs deliberately stop doing it.

Lore notices the contradiction:

> **Possible outdated convention detected**
>
> ProductInterface preference has been contradicted by three recently approved PRs.
>
> Review rule?

Now engineering documentation becomes somewhat **self-maintaining**.

That's much more compelling to me than "AI documentation".

---

# Your side-hustle MVP is surprisingly small

Do **not** initially build Jira, Slack, VSCode, Harvest, GitLab, dashboards, agents and everything else.

Build one incredibly good loop:

```text
GitHub App
     │
     ▼
Read merged PRs
     │
     ├── PR description
     ├── diff
     ├── review comments
     └── resolution
     │
     ▼
Extract engineering lessons
     │
     ▼
Knowledge database
     │
     ▼
CLI / MCP server
     │
     ▼
Codex / Claude / Cursor
```

That's it.

### Version 0.1

Install the GitHub app.

It analyses perhaps the previous 100 merged PRs.

Then it produces:

```text
I discovered 37 potential engineering conventions.

12 high confidence
18 medium confidence
7 low confidence
```

Developer approves/rejects them.

Now expose:

```text
lore context SS-6160
```

or an MCP equivalent.

The coding agent gets relevant knowledge before working.

That is something you could genuinely build yourself evenings/weekends.

