# And your node-to-node system fits too

Interestingly, even the decentralised system you've been thinking about could eventually become relevant.

Companies are going to be extremely sensitive about uploading:

```text
source code
PRs
Jira
architecture
customer information
internal decisions
```

So Lore could ultimately have a **local/private knowledge node**.

```text
Developer machine
      │
      ▼
Lore Node
      │
      ├── local embeddings
      ├── encrypted knowledge
      ├── repository access
      └── MCP
             │
             ▼
        AI coding agent

      │
 encrypted coordination
      ▼

Lore Cloud
identity / billing / sync / policy
```

The cloud doesn't necessarily need the company's source code.

That privacy architecture could become a substantial enterprise differentiator.

But **don't build this first**.

---

# One thing I would actively avoid

Don't make:

> "One dashboard containing Jira + GitHub + Harvest + AI."

You previously gravitated toward a Mission Control-style system, and there is something useful in it. But as a standalone business it's broad, expensive to build and easy for Jira/GitHub/Linear/etc. to absorb.

Instead make the dashboard an **output of the intelligence layer**.

Your valuable asset isn't the dashboard.

It's this:

```text
                    ORGANISATIONAL MEMORY
                            │
          ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐
          ▼                 ▼                 ▼
       Humans             Agents           Reports
          │                 │                 │
          ▼                 ▼                 ▼
       GitHub             Codex           Managers
       Jira               Claude
       IDE                Cursor
```

---

# Why I think this idea fits *you*

There's a notable pattern in the things you've asked me about.

You're repeatedly dealing with **information falling between systems**.

Jira says what should happen.

GitHub says what changed.

PR comments say how people want it changed.

The repository says what currently exists.

Slack explains something important once and then loses it.

Harvest says how long it took.

And your own memory has to join all of those together.

You've repeatedly tried to automate that joining process.

That is usually a much stronger source of business ideas than sitting down and asking:

> "What app could I make?"

You've experienced the problem.

---

## If I were in your position

I'd spend **£0 initially** other than API usage and hosting.

I'd build:

**`Lore — AI memory for software teams`**

with only:

1. GitHub installation.
2. Historical PR analysis.
3. Extraction of conventions/decisions/reviewer feedback.
4. Evidence attached to every learned rule.
5. Approve/reject/edit knowledge.
6. MCP server.
7. `get_context(task)` for Codex/Claude.
8. Automatic learning from subsequent PR reviews.

Then I'd install it against repositories I already work with and find out whether it starts surfacing things where I think:

> "Bloody hell, I'd forgotten about that."

If it does, you've found something.

If after a few weeks it merely generates obvious coding standards, kill or pivot it.

The **test isn't whether the AI works**.

The test is whether it remembers useful things that experienced engineers forgot.

That is the product.

And of all the ideas you've raised with me over time, **this is the one I'd personally investigate first as a serious side-business**, because your previous experiments, your day-to-day problems, current AI-development trends, and a plausible SaaS business model all intersect unusually cleanly here.

[1]: https://blog.jetbrains.com/research/2026/04/which-ai-coding-tools-do-developers-actually-use-at-work/?utm_source=chatgpt.com "Which AI Coding Tools Do Developers Actually Use at Work? - The JetBrains Blog"
[2]: https://www.reuters.com/technology/ai-code-review-platform-coderabbit-valued-15-billion-latest-funding-round-2026-08-12/?utm_source=chatgpt.com "AI code review platform CodeRabbit valued at $1.5 billion in latest funding round"
[3]: https://github.blog/ai-and-ml/github-copilot/60-million-copilot-code-reviews-and-counting/?utm_source=chatgpt.com "60 million Copilot code reviews and counting - The GitHub Blog"
[4]: https://www.techradar.com/pro/bad-news-your-ai-application-isnt-that-special?utm_source=chatgpt.com "Bad news: your AI application isn't that special"
[5]: https://www.techradar.com/pro/the-ai-era-is-creating-a-new-cto?utm_source=chatgpt.com "The AI era is creating a new CTO"
[6]: https://www.getharvest.com/integrations/jira?utm_source=chatgpt.com "Jira Time Tracking Integration - Harvest"



The key is: **do not rely on the AI remembering to ask Lore.** Make Lore part of the execution path around the AI.

The model should be treated as an unreliable decision-maker for *when* to fetch context. Your platform should deterministically decide when context must be loaded, what scope to search, and what knowledge is allowed to be stored.

## 1. AI should not decide whether Lore is used

A weak design would be:

```text
Codex
  ↓
"Maybe I should call Lore..."
  ↓
MCP tool
```

That will eventually fail because the model may decide it already has enough context.

Instead:

```text
Task
  ↓
Lore Orchestrator
  ↓
Mandatory context discovery
  ↓
Context package
  ↓
AI agent
  ↓
Code changes
  ↓
Mandatory impact/safety review
  ↓
PR
  ↓
Human review
  ↓
Knowledge extraction
```

The AI never gets a task without Lore being involved.

MCP can still be used for deeper queries, but that's **secondary retrieval**, not the primary safeguard.

---

# 2. How Lore knows what knowledge might affect some code

You need an **impact graph** rather than simply embeddings.

Suppose the task is:

> SS-6160: change the Avalara ShipFrom/ShipTo payload.

Lore first resolves tangible entities:

```json
{
  "ticket": "SS-6160",
  "concepts": [
    "Avalara",
    "ShipFrom",
    "ShipTo",
    "tax",
    "transaction"
  ]
}
```

Then the repository analyser finds likely code:

```text
src/Tax/AvalaraClient.php
src/Tax/TransactionMapper.php
src/Order/Address.php
tests/Tax/AvalaraClientTest.php
```

But importantly, it expands from there.

For every file it understands:

```text
imports
implements
extends
calls
called-by
reads-table
writes-table
uses-config
uses-event
produces-payload
tests
historically-changed-with
owned-by
related-tickets
```

You end up with something like:

```text
SS-6160
   │
   ▼
AvalaraTransactionMapper
   │
   ├── calls → AddressMapper
   │
   ├── produces → Avalara API payload
   │
   ├── tested-by → AvalaraTransactionMapperTest
   │
   └── used-by → CreateTaxTransaction
                         │
                         └── called-by → Checkout
```

That graph lets Lore reason about impact.

---

# 3. The graph should combine static and historical relationships

Static analysis alone won't discover everything.

You want several layers.

### Static code relationships

For PHP, for example:

```text
class → interface
class → parent
method → method
method → service
service → repository
repository → database table
controller → service
event → listener
configuration → consumer
```

These are relatively deterministic.

### Git relationships

Git gives very valuable implicit architecture:

```text
file A changes with file B 87% of the time
class X was introduced alongside class Y
changes to service Z usually change test Q
```

For example:

```text
TransactionMapper.php
   ↓ co-change 91%
TransactionMapperTest.php
```

Lore should therefore consider the test likely affected even before an LLM says so.

### Jira/PR relationships

Then:

```text
SS-6160
   ↓
PR #842
   ↓
files changed
   ↓
review comments
   ↓
approved implementation
```

Over time, that becomes an extremely rich graph.

---

# 4. Then AI helps where static analysis cannot

AI should add **semantic edges**.

For example it may observe:

```php
if ($this->isOmsBasket()) {
    return;
}
```

Static analysis can tell you where `isOmsBasket()` comes from.

AI can infer:

```text
Purpose:
Preserves legacy basket-search behaviour.

Business area:
CP/POS basket.

Risk:
Removing this may hide inactive products.
```

But this should initially be considered an **inference**, not fact.

Store that distinction explicitly.

```json
{
  "statement": "This branch preserves legacy CP/POS basket behaviour",
  "type": "inference",
  "confidence": 0.61
}
```

If Jira + PR discussion confirm it:

```json
{
  "statement": "This branch preserves legacy CP/POS basket behaviour",
  "type": "verified_decision",
  "confidence": 0.98,
  "evidence": [
    "JIRA:SS-1234",
    "GH:PR-842",
    "GH:COMMENT-19192"
  ]
}
```

That distinction is critical.

---

# 5. How Lore knows what to ask

The AI doesn't start with an open-ended:

> What might be relevant?

The orchestrator generates structured questions from the task.

For every task it asks internally:

```text
What files are explicitly referenced?

What symbols are involved?

What calls those symbols?

What do those symbols call?

What interfaces/contracts are involved?

What tests cover those symbols?

What external APIs are involved?

What database tables are involved?

What previous tickets changed these symbols?

What previous PR review feedback concerned them?

What organisational rules apply to this repository/path/symbol?

What previous regressions involved this area?

What files historically change alongside these files?
```

That can largely happen **without an LLM**.

Then an LLM can perform a second semantic pass:

> Given this ticket and discovered dependency graph, are there concepts we appear to have missed?

That's a much safer use of AI.

---

# 6. Context should progressively expand

You don't want to dump the company's entire knowledge base into every prompt.

Use something like:

```text
TASK
 ↓
repository
 ↓
candidate files
 ↓
symbols
 ↓
dependencies
 ↓
historical relationships
 ↓
applicable knowledge
```

Then rank context.

Example:

```text
HIGH

Rule #119
Transaction mappers must implement TransactionMapperInterface.
Confidence: 0.99
Evidence: 14 PRs

Decision #42
Avalara address roles require independent address codes.
Confidence: 1.0
Evidence: SS-6160

MEDIUM

Reviewer preference #12
Joe generally prefers DTOs for external API payloads.
Confidence: 0.77

LOW

Possible convention:
Tax mappers tend to keep formatting outside API clients.
Confidence: 0.53
```

I'd probably only inject high-confidence information automatically.

Medium-confidence material can be marked as advisory.

---

# 7. How you force agents to use Lore consistently

This is possibly the most important architectural decision.

There are several enforcement levels.

## Weak

Give developers an MCP server:

```text
lore.search
lore.get_context
```

Useful, but not reliable.

---

## Better

Supply permanent agent instructions:

```text
Before modifying code:
1. call lore.prepare_task
2. examine returned constraints
3. call lore.check_change before completion
```

Better, but still ultimately model-controlled.

---

## Strong

Wrap the coding agent.

Instead of developers executing:

```bash
codex
```

they execute:

```bash
lore codex
```

Lore controls the session:

```text
lore
 ├── reads task
 ├── analyses repository
 ├── builds mandatory context
 ├── starts Codex
 ├── exposes MCP
 ├── watches modified files
 ├── recomputes affected context
 └── runs completion review
```

Now the developer doesn't need to trust the model.

---

# 8. Watching files is important

Suppose Codex initially thinks it's only changing:

```text
src/Avalara/TransactionMapper.php
```

But halfway through it edits:

```text
src/Order/Order.php
```

Lore should detect that.

Immediately:

```text
new file touched
    ↓
extract symbols
    ↓
expand graph
    ↓
find applicable rules
    ↓
inject additional context
```

For example:

> **Additional constraint discovered**
>
> `Order::getDeliveryAddress()` is also consumed by the BC integration.
>
> Avoid changing its return semantics.
>
> Related decision: BC-2382.

This is where the system starts becoming genuinely powerful.

---

# 9. Do another deterministic check before completion

When the agent says:

> Done.

Lore shouldn't simply accept that.

It sees:

```text
git diff
changed symbols
dependency graph
tests
known rules
task requirements
```

and runs:

```text
lore verify
```

Producing something like:

```text
CHANGE SAFETY REPORT

Task:
SS-6160

Changed:
- AvalaraTransactionMapper::mapAddresses()
- AddressCode::fromRole()

Potentially affected:
- CreateTaxTransaction
- RefundTaxTransaction
- AvalaraTransactionMapperTest

Applicable rules:
✓ Address roles must have independent codes
✓ API clients must not construct address payloads directly

Warnings:
! RefundTaxTransaction also consumes AddressCode
  but its tests were not modified or executed.

Historical regression:
! PR #782 previously broke refund tax requests
  after modifying address-role mapping.

Recommendation:
Run RefundTaxTransactionTest.
```

This should happen **outside the agent**.

---

# 10. Safety reports should not automatically become knowledge

This is another crucial distinction.

You need separate stores for:

```text
Observations
Knowledge
```

An observation might be:

```json
{
  "event": "review_comment",
  "reviewer": "joe",
  "text": "Can we use the interface here?",
  "pr": 1271
}
```

That is evidence.

It isn't yet a company rule.

---

# 11. Knowledge promotion pipeline

I'd design it something like:

```text
Raw event
   ↓
Extract candidate lesson
   ↓
Find corroborating evidence
   ↓
Determine scope
   ↓
Detect contradictions
   ↓
Calculate confidence
   ↓
Candidate knowledge
   ↓
Human approval OR sufficient evidence
   ↓
Active knowledge
```

For example:

### First observation

Joe writes:

> Use `ProductRepositoryInterface`.

Lore creates:

```text
Candidate:
Prefer repository interfaces.

Evidence: 1
Confidence: 0.31
Scope: unknown
```

Don't inject it as a rule.

### Five similar reviews

```text
Evidence: 5
Repositories: 3
Confidence: 0.72
Likely scope: team
```

Now it can appear as advisory context.

### Fifteen examples + approvals

```text
Evidence: 15
Contradictions: 0
Confidence: 0.96
```

Now you can potentially promote it to:

```text
Established convention
```

---

# 12. Never let the AI silently rewrite organisational knowledge

I would make this an absolute rule.

The AI can propose:

```text
CREATE
UPDATE
DEPRECATE
MERGE
CONTRADICT
```

But the knowledge service performs the actual mutation.

For example:

```json
{
  "operation": "propose",
  "candidate": {
    "kind": "engineering_convention",
    "statement": "Repositories should depend on interfaces",
    "scope": {
      "repository": "soho/ecom"
    },
    "evidence": [
      "github:pr:123",
      "github:review:456"
    ]
  }
}
```

The backend validates:

```text
Are the sources real?
Does the evidence contain this claim?
Is scope justified?
Does existing knowledge contradict it?
Is this someone's preference or an actual rule?
Is there enough evidence?
```

The model itself gets no direct DB write access.

---

# 13. Provenance must exist everywhere

Every piece of knowledge should answer:

> **Why do you believe this?**

Something like:

```json
{
  "id": "knowledge_92",
  "statement": "OMS basket search intentionally skips Active filtering",
  "scope": {
    "repository": "soho/ecom",
    "paths": [
      "src/Search/Basket/*"
    ]
  },
  "classification": "architectural_decision",
  "confidence": 0.97,

  "evidence": [
    {
      "type": "jira",
      "id": "SS-2189"
    },
    {
      "type": "pull_request",
      "id": "GH-918"
    },
    {
      "type": "review_comment",
      "id": "GH-918-COMMENT-42"
    }
  ],

  "created": "2026-08-15T...",
  "last_confirmed": "...",

  "status": "active"
}
```

No evidence means it should generally be treated as low-confidence AI inference.

---

# 14. I'd distinguish five knowledge classes

This avoids one of the biggest dangers: mixing facts with opinions.

```text
FACT
Something objectively true about the system.

DECISION
Something intentionally designed this way.

RULE
Something developers are expected to follow.

PREFERENCE
Something a reviewer/team tends to prefer.

INFERENCE
Something Lore thinks is probably true.
```

Example:

```text
FACT
OrderRepository implements OrderRepositoryInterface.

DECISION
OMS basket search deliberately doesn't use the normal active filter.

RULE
Payment classes must not log card details.

PREFERENCE
Joe normally prefers dependencies typed against interfaces.

INFERENCE
This service appears to be the canonical path for order refunds.
```

They should be treated very differently.

---

# 15. Safety rules need a stronger level

Some knowledge should never be statistical.

For example:

```text
Never log access tokens.
Never change payment status without...
Never query production from tests.
Never commit credentials.
```

Store them as:

```text
policy
```

rather than learned knowledge.

Policies should be deterministic and manually controlled.

```text
Policy
   ↓
mandatory check
   ↓
failure blocks completion
```

Whereas:

```text
Reviewer preference
   ↓
advisory warning
```

doesn't block anything.

---

# 16. A useful enforcement hierarchy

I'd use something like:

| Type                 | Effect         |
| -------------------- | -------------- |
| Security policy      | Block          |
| Compliance rule      | Block          |
| Architecture rule    | Error/warning  |
| Established decision | Strong warning |
| Convention           | Guidance       |
| Reviewer preference  | Suggestion     |
| AI inference         | Informational  |

That prevents Lore becoming an infuriating linting system.

---

# 17. Knowledge should also decay

Something that was correct in 2024 may be wrong in 2027.

So knowledge needs lifecycle:

```text
candidate
    ↓
active
    ↓
challenged
    ↓
superseded
    ↓
archived
```

And confidence decay.

For example:

```text
Last evidence: 2 years ago
Repository architecture substantially changed
Relevant files deleted

0.94 → 0.68
```

Then:

> This convention may be stale.

---

# 18. Contradictions are valuable

Suppose Lore knows:

```text
Use repositories through interfaces.
```

Then sees three approved PRs using concrete implementations.

Don't overwrite the rule.

Create:

```text
CONTRADICTION

Existing:
Repository dependencies use interfaces.

New evidence:
PR #912
PR #943
PR #961

Possible explanations:
- convention changed
- exception exists
- narrower scope required
```

Then the human can resolve:

```text
New actual rule:

Use interfaces for application services.
Concrete repositories are permitted inside migration tooling.
```

Lore becomes **more precise over time**, rather than simply accumulating garbage.

---

# 19. The complete loop

I'd ultimately architect it like this:

```text
                    ┌────────────────────┐
                    │ Jira / GitHub Task │
                    └─────────┬──────────┘
                              │
                              ▼
                    ┌────────────────────┐
                    │ Task Understanding │
                    └─────────┬──────────┘
                              │
                              ▼
                    ┌────────────────────┐
                    │   Impact Engine    │
                    │                    │
                    │ AST                │
                    │ Dependency graph   │
                    │ Git history        │
                    │ PR/Jira history    │
                    └─────────┬──────────┘
                              │
                              ▼
                    ┌────────────────────┐
                    │ Knowledge Resolver │
                    └─────────┬──────────┘
                              │
                     mandatory context
                              │
                              ▼
                        ┌──────────┐
                        │ AI Agent │
                        └────┬─────┘
                             │
                     edits repository
                             │
                             ▼
                    ┌────────────────────┐
                    │ Change Observer    │
                    │ re-evaluate graph  │
                    └─────────┬──────────┘
                              │
                         more context
                              │
                              ▼
                         ┌─────────┐
                         │  Agent  │
                         └────┬────┘
                              │
                              ▼
                    ┌────────────────────┐
                    │ Safety / Impact    │
                    │ Review             │
                    └─────────┬──────────┘
                              │
                              ▼
                             PR
                              │
                 ┌────────────┴────────────┐
                 │                         │
                 ▼                         ▼
          Human review                 CI/tests
                 │                         │
                 └────────────┬────────────┘
                              ▼
                    ┌────────────────────┐
                    │ Evidence Collector │
                    └─────────┬──────────┘
                              ▼
                    ┌────────────────────┐
                    │ Knowledge Extractor│
                    └─────────┬──────────┘
                              ▼
                    ┌────────────────────┐
                    │ Validation /       │
                    │ Promotion Engine   │
                    └─────────┬──────────┘
                              ▼
                       Knowledge Graph
```

---

# The most important product principle

I would write this into the project's architecture from day one:

> **AI proposes; deterministic systems discover and enforce; evidence determines what becomes knowledge.**

That solves most of the concerns you're raising.

You're not trying to create a magical AI that remembers everything and always behaves correctly.

You're creating a controlled system where the AI is one component:

```text
AI:
understands semantics
extracts meaning
suggests relationships
explains risk

Software:
tracks dependencies
forces retrieval
records evidence
controls writes
enforces policy
maintains history
```

And that separation is what would make **Lore much more credible as an engineering product** rather than another "give an LLM access to GitHub and hope for the best" application.
